.TH "ZPCPKCS11.CONF" "5" "2026" "LIBZPC v2" ""
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
zpcpkcs11.conf - Configuration file for the \f[V]zpcpkcs11\f[R] PKCS#11
library
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
This page documents the format of the configuration file read by the
\f[V]zpcpkcs11\f[R] PKCS#11 library on initialization.
The configuration file maps PKCS#11 key labels to OpenSSL URIs that
reference the corresponding protected-key origin files.
Each entry becomes a PKCS#11 object accessible to applications that load
\f[V]zpcpkcs11.so\f[R] as their PKCS#11 provider.
.SH FILE LOCATION
.PP
The default configuration file path is:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
/etc/zpcpkcs11/zpcpkcs11.conf
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The path can be overridden by setting the environment variable
\f[B]ZPCPKCS11_CONFIG\f[R] to an alternative file path.
The environment variable is read using \f[I]secure_getenv\f[R](3), so it
is ignored in set-user-ID and set-group-ID programs.
.SH FILE FORMAT
.PP
The configuration file is a plain-text file.
Each non-empty, non-comment line specifies one key entry with the
following syntax:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
<label>=<uri>
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[I]label\f[R]
An arbitrary string that identifies the key.
It is used as the PKCS#11 \f[I]CKA_LABEL\f[R] attribute of the resulting
key objects.
The label must not contain \f[V]=\f[R], space, or newline characters.
.TP
\f[I]uri\f[R]
An OpenSSL URI that references the key material.
The URI is passed to the OpenSSL store loader
(\f[I]OSSL_STORE_open_ex\f[R](3ossl)).
Supported URI schemes depend on the configured OpenSSL store loaders;
the most common forms are:
.RS
.IP \[bu] 2
A plain file path (e.g.\ \f[V]/path/to/mykey.pem\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
A \f[V]file:\f[R] URI (e.g.\ \f[V]file:///path/to/mykey.pem\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
An \f[V]hbkzpc:\f[R] URI as described in hbkzpcprovider(7).
.PP
The referenced object may be a protected-key origin private key (or
keypair), a clear private key PEM file, or a public-key-only PEM file.
When a private key or keypair is loaded, both a private-key object and
(if public material is present) a public-key object are created.
When a public-key-only PEM file is loaded, only a public-key object is
created.
Keys with unsupported algorithms are skipped with a diagnostic message
written to \f[I]stderr\f[R].
.RE
.PP
\f[B]Comment lines\f[R] start with \f[V]#\f[R] in the first column and
are ignored.
.PP
\f[B]Blank lines\f[R] (empty or containing only a newline) are ignored.
.PP
Any line that does not match the \f[V]<label>=<uri>\f[R] syntax is
treated as a syntax error.
Processing stops at the first syntax error and \f[V]C_Initialize\f[R](3)
returns \f[V]CKR_FUNCTION_FAILED\f[R].
.SH SUPPORTED KEY TYPES
.PP
The library loads EC and Edwards-curve keys.
Depending on the type of key material referenced by the URI, it creates
private-key objects, public-key objects, or both (see \f[I]PKCS#11
OBJECTS\f[R] below).
The following algorithms are supported:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]ECDSA\f[R]: prime256v1 (P-256), secp384r1 (P-384), secp521r1
(P-521)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]EdDSA\f[R]: ED25519, ED448
.PP
Keys of any other type produce an error message on \f[I]stderr\f[R] and
are skipped; the remaining entries in the configuration file continue to
be processed.
.SH PKCS#11 OBJECTS
.PP
For each successfully loaded key, the library creates one or two PKCS#11
objects in the single token provided by \f[V]zpcpkcs11\f[R], depending
on the type of key material referenced by the URI:
.IP \[bu] 2
A \f[B]private key\f[R] object (\f[V]CKO_PRIVATE_KEY\f[R]): created when
the URI references a private key or keypair (e.g.\ an \f[V]hbkzpc:\f[R]
URI or a clear private-key PEM file).
.IP \[bu] 2
A \f[B]public key\f[R] object (\f[V]CKO_PUBLIC_KEY\f[R]): created when
public-key material is available (i.e.\ always for public-key-only PEM
files, and for private-key PEM files or \f[V]hbkzpc:\f[R] URIs that
include the public portion).
.PP
Both objects share the same \f[V]CKA_LABEL\f[R] (the \f[I]label\f[R]
from the config entry) and the same \f[V]CKA_ID\f[R] (derived from the
line number of the entry in the configuration file).
.PP
The token is read-only: \f[V]C_CreateObject\f[R],
\f[V]C_CopyObject\f[R], \f[V]C_DestroyObject\f[R], and
\f[V]C_SetAttributeValue\f[R] all return
\f[V]CKR_TOKEN_WRITE_PROTECTED\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
A minimal configuration file with two keys:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Signing key for service A
service-a-key = /etc/zpcpkcs11/keys/service-a.pem

# Signing key for service B (using an hbkzpc URI)
service-b-key = hbkzpc:origin-type=uv;origin-alg=1.2.840.10045.3.1.7;origin-blob=0102...
\f[R]
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]ZPCPKCS11_CONFIG\f[R]
If set, overrides the default configuration file path
\f[V]/etc/zpcpkcs11/zpcpkcs11.conf\f[R].
Ignored in privileged processes (see \f[I]secure_getenv\f[R](3)).
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[V]/etc/zpcpkcs11/zpcpkcs11.conf\f[R]
Default configuration file.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
hbkzpcprovider(7), hbkzpcprovider.conf(5), zpckey(1),
\f[I]secure_getenv\f[R](3), \f[I]OSSL_STORE_open_ex\f[R](3ossl).
